INT. PRETTY PINK NAILS - LATE AFTERNOON

Rain taps the front window. The sign buzzes. A string of dusty Christmas lights, still up in March, blinks out of sync.

PAM, 40s, nail tech, bends over a client's hand with a drill file. LOU, 50s, the client, scrolls her phone with her free hand.

The smell of acetone. Of something sweeter under it.

PAM
Your cuticles are in rough shape Lou.

LOU
It's the dishwasher. Always the dishwasher.

PAM
You need a oil. I'll put some on after.

The drill WHIRS. Stops. Whirs.

Outside a bus hisses past. Wet tires on wet road.

LOU
Is it me or is it cold in here?

PAM
Heater's been broke since Tuesday. Mrs K says shes getting a guy.

LOU
A guy.

PAM
A guy.

Pause. The lights hum. Somewhere in the back a faucet drips. Drip. Drip.

LOU
Did you hear that?

PAM
The faucet?

LOU
No. Under it.

They listen. The faucet. The rain. The sign.

Then a slow scraping, like a chair being dragged across tile in the back room.

PAM
(too brightly)
Old building. Pipes.

The bell on the front door JINGLES.

GUS, 60s, a tall man in a soaked grey raincoat with no umbrella, steps in. Water runs off his sleeves onto the mat. He smiles with too many teeth.

GUS
Afternoon, ladies. Is the owner in?

PAM
Mrs K's out. Can I help you?

GUS
I'll wait.

He sits in the pedicure chair by the window. The water jets turn on by themselves. Nobody touches them.

LOU
(whispering)
Pam.

PAM
I see it.

The scraping in the back stops.

Rain. Rain. Rain.

GUS
Nice weather for it.
